al arm/fixed 86299.55574229342 85300.9990268123 net diff 3939.4614640629443\n10y net rate 0.0975 payment diff 6780.913359464583 bal arm/fixed 87150.64564731077 85300.9990268123 net diff 8630.559979963058"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":44,"aggregated":"0.0575 583.5728564435492 92762.18684092912\n0.0675 648.5980965682157 93875.70953727998\nbal arm after 60 92762.18684092912 savings monthly vs fixed\nfixed p/bal 648.5980965682157 93875.70953727998 cum payment diff 3901.5144074799923 bal diff fixed-arm 1113.5226963508612\n0.0375 476.9193439826431 diff from fixed -171.67875258557262\n0.0475 528.8533319769042 diff from fixed -119.74476459131154\n0.0575 583.5728564435491 diff from fixed -65.02524012466665\n0.0675 640.9046399232689 diff from fixed -7.6934566449468775\n0.0775 700.6594777371592 diff from fixed 52.06138116894351\n0.0875 762.6384158359126 diff from fixed 114.04031926769687\n0.0975 826.6385593506254 diff from fixed 178.0404627824097\n0.1075 892.4582488869852 diff from fixed 243.86015231876945\n10y rate 0.0575 arm pays 70028.74277322591 fixed pays 77831.77158818589 diff arm-fixed -7803.02881495998\n10y rate 0.0675 arm pays 73468.64978200909 fixed pays 77831.77158818589 diff arm-fixed -4363.121806176801\n10y rate 0.0775 arm pays 77053.9400508425 fixed pays 77831.77158818589 diff arm-fixed -777.831537343387\n10y rate 0.0875 arm pays 80772.6763367677 fixed pays 77831.77158818589 diff arm-fixed 2940.9047485818155\n10y rate 0.0975 arm pays 84612.68494765047 fixed pays 77831.77158818589 diff arm-fixed 6780.913359464583\nequal payment reset rate 0.06880988021135617\n10y net rate 0.0575 payment diff -7803.02881495998 bal arm/fixed 83120.14880012455 85300.9990268123 net diff -9983.879041647713\n10y net rate 0.0675 payment diff -4363.121806176801 bal arm/fixed 84289.1867176863 85300.9990268123 net diff -5374.934115302807\n10y net rate 0.0775 payment diff -777.831537343387 bal arm/fixed 85347.54895734502 85300.9990268123 net diff -731.2816068106622\n10y net rate 0.0875 payment diff 2940.9047485818155 bal arm/fixed 86299.55574229342 85300.9990268123 net diff 3939.4614640629443\n10y net rate 0.0975 payment diff 6780.913359464583 bal arm/fixed 87150.64564731077 85300.9990268123 net diff 8630.559979963058","cwd":"/home/bpwonka/clawd-ecarg-deep"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1781158342039}}
{"type":"message","id":"730ef493","parentId":"c20fa057","timestamp":"2026-06-11T06:12:45.417Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Completed mortgage comparison. Key findings for main agent:\n\n**Assumptions used:** $100,000 loan amount, 30-year amortization, no points/closing-cost differences included, ARM fixed for first 5 years then adjusts every 6 months. Results scale linearly: multiply by loan amount / 100,000.\n\n### Payment comparison per $100,000 borrowed\n\n| Loan | Initial Rate | Monthly P&I |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| 5/6 ARM | 5.75% | **$583.57** |\n| 30-year fixed | 6.75% | **$648.60** |\n\n**Initial monthly savings with ARM:** about **$65.03 per $100k**  \nExamples:\n- $400k loan: ~$260/month savings\n- $600k loan: ~$390/month savings\n- $800k loan: ~$520/month savings\n\n### First 5 years\n\nOver the first 60 months, per $100k:\n\n- ARM payments: **$35,014**\n- Fixed payments: **$38,916**\n- ARM saves in payments: **$3,902**\n- ARM also has a lower balance after 5 years:\n  - ARM balance: **$92,762**\n  - Fixed balance: **$93,876**\n  - ARM owes **$1,114 less**\n\nSo if the borrower sells/refinances before the first adjustment, the ARM is clearly cheaper by roughly **$5,015 per $100k** including both payment savings and lower remaining balance.\n\n### After year 5: reset sensitivity\n\nAt month 61, the ARM payment resets based on the remaining balance and the new rate. Approximate new ARM payment per $100k original loan:\n\n| Reset Rate | New ARM Payment | Difference vs Fixed Payment |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 5.75% | $583.57 | -$65/mo |\n| 6.75% | $640.90 | -$8/mo |\n| 7.75% | $700.66 | +$52/mo |\n| 8.75% | $762.64 | +$114/mo |\n| 9.75% | $826.64 | +$178/mo |\n| 10.75% | $892.46 | +$244/mo |\n\nThe first reset rate where the ARM payment roughly equals the fixed payment is about **6.88%**. Below that, the ARM payment remains lower; above that, the fixed loan starts having the lower monthly payment.\n\n### 10-year scenario comparison\n\nAssuming the ARM resets once at year 5 and then stays at that rate through year 10:\n\n| ARM Reset Rate Years 6–10 | Payment Difference vs Fixed Over 10 Years | Net Difference Including Balance |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 5.75% | ARM saves $7,803 | ARM better by $9,984 |\n| 6.75% | ARM saves $4,363 | ARM better by $5,375 |\n| 7.75% | ARM saves $778 | ARM better by $731 |\n| 8.75% | ARM costs $2,941 more | ARM worse by $3,939 |\n| 9.75% | ARM costs $6,781 more | ARM worse by $8,631 |\n\nBreak-even over a 10-year hold is somewhere a bit below **8%** for the post-reset rate. If rates after year 5 are around 7.75%, the ARM still slightly wins due to the first five years of savings. If rates jump to 8.75% or higher and stay there, the fixed loan becomes better.\n\n### Strategic takeaways\n\n**5/6 ARM at 5.75% is attractive if:**\n- Borrower expects to sell or refinance within 5–7 years.\n- Borrower can tolerate payment risk after year 5.\n- There are meaningful upfront savings and no excessive ARM fees/points.\n- Borrower believes rates may fall or be similar by the adjustment period.\n- The ARM has reasonable caps, e.g. 2/1/5 or similar.\n\n**30-year fixed at 6.75% is better if:**\n- Borrower plans to keep the home/mortgage long-term.\n- Payment certainty matters more than initial savings.\n- Borrower would be financially stressed by a reset to 8–10%+.\n- ARM has harsh caps, margin, or fees.\n- Borrower doesn’t want refinance risk.\n\n### Important variables to check before deciding\n\nNeed actual loan disclosures for:\n- ARM index, likely SOFR or Treasury-based.\n- Margin.\n- Initial adjustment cap.\n- Subsequent 6-month cap.\n- Lifetime cap.\n- Points, lender credits, origination fees.\n- Prepayment penalty, if any.\n- Whether the 5.75% ARM has higher closing costs than the fixed.\n\n### Bottom line\n\nFor any borrower likely to move/refinance within 5 years, the **5/6 ARM at 5.75% is likely financially superior**. For a long-term hold, the choice depends on risk tolerance and ARM caps. The ARM keeps winning if post-reset rates are moderate, but the **30-year fixed is the safer option** if the borrower wants certainty or could not handle a significant payment increase."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":6129,"output":1203,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":7332,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1781158342042}}
